{
	"_comment": "Bulgarian. Suffixed definites, NO indefinite article (indefinite_none). Modern Bulgarian has no infinitive, so gram.verb is keyed by the IMPERFECTIVE verbal noun (отглаголно съществително — изтриване, писане): that string is what gram.word bridges to, what Progress() titles ('Изтриване...'), and what ActionFailed() inserts verbatim ('Неуспешно: бутане клон') — matching how Bulgarian software headlines actually read, since a finite 1st-person present ('изтривам') would sit oddly in that slot. The past slot carries the past PASSIVE PARTICIPLE, agreed by gender via the declared +а/+о rule; where aspect naturally splits (writing vs written) the participle is drawn from the perfective partner (написан, from perfective напиша) while the gerund stays imperfective (писане, from пиша) — this is how Bulgarian actually works, not an inconsistency. Masculine definite singular uses only the FULL/subject-position article (пълен член, -ът/-ят: файлът); the short/object-position form (-а/-я: виждам файла) is not modelled — a documented v1 gap, matching this schema's stated simplification for Bulgarian. The single definite_suffix_plural rule (+те) correctly fits plurals ending in -и or -е (файлове→файловете, задачи→задачите); neuter (and other) plurals ending in -а/-я take a DIFFERENT marker (+та: съобщения→съобщенията, not +те) that this one-rule schema cannot express — съобщение's plural definite is therefore deliberately not exercised or tested. Masculine nouns also take a distinct numeral COUNT form after a number (бройна форма: '5 файла', not '5 файлове') that this schema has no separate slot for; count tests use feminine nouns (задача, грешка) where singular/plural is unambiguous and no such split exists. 'push' and 'commit' render their nearest general sense (бутам = to push; записвам = to record) rather than assumed git jargon. Nominative-only; no case declension.",
